# What `docker build .` must not send to the daemon.
#
# There was no .dockerignore, and the build context is `.` - so every build uploaded the whole
# working tree before the first instruction ran: `.venv/` (a full CUDA torch install), `.git/`,
# `.dvc/cache/`, `mlruns/`, `data/`, `experiments/` with its 1.3 GB of checkpoints, and ~90 MB of
# loose ultralytics weights at the repo root. The Dockerfile copies four things - `requirements/`,
# `src/`, `schemas/` and `pyproject.toml` - so none of the rest was ever used for anything.
#
# It is not free: the CI `image` job already has to `rm -rf /usr/share/dotnet` and friends to fit
# a CUDA image on a runner, and it now builds on every push.
#
# Written as a deny-list rather than `*` plus un-ignores, because the Dockerfile's COPY lines are
# the real allow-list and keeping a second one here is a second thing to forget to update.

# Environments and version control
.venv/
venv/
venv-labelstudio/
.git/
.github/
.gitattributes
.gitignore

# Caches - regenerated, and large
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Data, models and experiment output. The image mounts `data/` and `experiments/` read-only at
# run time (see docker-compose.yml); it must not bake them in.
data/
experiments/
mlruns/
.dvc/cache/
runs/
labeling/

# Loose ultralytics checkpoints. They are gitignored by `*.pt` and therefore invisible to
# `git status`, which is exactly why they sat in the build context unnoticed.
*.pt
*.pth
*.onnx

# Everything a build does not read
tests/
docs/
notebooks/
reports/
configs/
scripts/
app/
Makefile
tasks.ps1
env.yml
dvc.yaml
dvc.lock
*.md
